(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Machine Learning & AI"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Machine Learning & AI"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Machine Learning and AI: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nShow every step and state units or assumptions. All numerical answers below were computed and checked with NumPy, scikit-learn or PyTorch. Where a question says \"in code\" write a short Python function and test it on the given data.\n\n# Assignment 1: Regression, Gradient Descent and Regularization (60 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Least squares (14 marks)** Data (x, y): (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 4), (3, 8), (4, 9). (a) Form XᵀX and Xᵀy for the model ŷ = b + w x [3]. (b) Solve the normal equations for b and w [3]. (c) Compute the residuals, the MSE and R² [5]. (d) Predict y at x = 5 and say why that prediction is an extrapolation risk [3].\n\n**Q2. Gradient descent (12 marks)** Use the data of Q1. (a) Write the gradient of J = (1/N) Σ (b + w x - y)² [3]. (b) Starting from b = w = 0 with η = 0.05, perform two full-batch steps [5]. (c) Explain why standardizing x would help and give the standardized x values (population standard deviation) [4].\n\n**Q3. Regularization (12 marks)** (a) For the data of Q1 find the ridge slope and intercept for λ = 2 and λ = 10, penalizing only the slope [6]. (b) OLS coefficients (3, -0.4, 1.2) on orthonormal features are fitted with an L1 penalty λ = 0.5. Give the lasso solution [3]. (c) Explain how ridge and lasso differ in the kind of model they produce [3].\n\n**Q4. Validation (10 marks)** (a) Perform leave-one-out cross-validation on the data of Q1 and compute the LOO MSE [7]. (b) Compare it with the training MSE and explain the difference [3].\n\n**Q5. Bias and variance (6 marks)** A regressor has squared bias 0.04, variance 0.09 and the data noise has standard deviation 0.3. (a) Find the expected squared test error [2]. (b) The model is changed so the bias falls to 0.01 but the variance rises to 0.20. Is that an improvement? [2] (c) Name two ways to reduce variance without changing the model class [2].\n\n**Q6. In code (6 marks)** Write a function fit_ridge(X, y, lam) that returns the weights using the closed form, and explain how you would choose lam.\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) With the column of ones, XᵀX = [[5, 10], [10, 30]] and Xᵀy = [25, 71].\n(b) Solve 5b + 10w = 25 and 10b + 30w = 71. Multiplying the first by 2 and subtracting: 10w = 21, so **w = 2.1** and b = (25 - 21)/5 = **0.8**.\n(c) Fitted values: 0.8, 2.9, 5.0, 7.1, 9.2. Residuals y - ŷ: **0.2, 0.1, -1.0, 0.9, -0.2** (they sum to 0). SS_res = 0.04 + 0.01 + 1.00 + 0.81 + 0.04 = 1.90, **MSE = 0.38**. ȳ = 5, SS_tot = 16 + 4 + 1 + 9 + 16 = 46, so **R² = 1 - 1.9/46 = 0.959**.\n(d) ŷ(5) = 0.8 + 10.5 = **11.3**. The data only cover x from 0 to 4; the linear trend may not continue (saturation, curvature), and there is no evidence about the region beyond the training range.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) ∂J/∂b = (2/N) Σ (ŷ - y) and ∂J/∂w = (2/N) Σ (ŷ - y) x.\n(b) Step 1: At b = w = 0 the errors ŷ - y are -1, -3, -4, -8, -9, whose sum is -25, so ∂J/∂b = (2/5)(-25) = -10. Σ(ŷ - y)x = 0 - 3 - 8 - 24 - 36 = -71, so ∂J/∂w = -28.4.\nStep 2: Update: **b = 0.5, w = 1.42**.\nStep 3: Second step: ŷ = 0.5 + 1.42x = 0.5, 1.92, 3.34, 4.76, 6.18; errors -0.5, -1.08, -0.66, -3.24, -2.82 with sum -8.3 and Σ error·x = -1.08 - 1.32 - 9.72 - 11.28 = -23.4.\nStep 4: Gradients -3.32 and -9.36.\nStep 5: Update: **b = 0.666, w = 1.888**, moving toward (0.8, 2.1).\n(c) The unscaled x makes the w-direction steep and the b-direction shallow (curvature of XᵀX has widely different eigenvalues), forcing a small η and slow progress. The mean of x is 2 and the population standard deviation is √2 = 1.414, so z = **-1.414, -0.707, 0, 0.707, 1.414**.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: Centered: x̄ = 2, ȳ = 5, S_xy = 21, S_xx = 10.\nStep 2: Ridge slope = S_xy/(S_xx + λ): for **λ = 2, slope 1.75 and intercept 5 - 1.75 × 2 = 1.5**; for **λ = 10, slope 1.05 and intercept 2.9**.\nStep 3: The slope shrinks toward zero as λ grows and the line still passes through (x̄, ȳ).\n(b) Soft-threshold each coefficient by 0.5: **(2.5, 0, 0.7)**.\n(c) Ridge shrinks all weights smoothly and rarely makes any exactly zero; it handles correlated features by sharing weight. Lasso sets some weights exactly to zero, producing a sparse, more interpretable model that performs feature selection.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: Leave out each point, refit, predict the held-out point.\nStep 2: Held-out predictions and squared errors: x = 0: 0.5 (error² 0.25); x = 1: 2.857 (0.0204); x = 2: 5.25 (1.5625); x = 3: 6.714 (1.6531); x = 4: 9.5 (0.25).\nStep 3: Mean: **LOO MSE = 0.747**.\n(b) The training MSE (0.38) is optimistic because each point helps fit the line that predicts it; LOO is nearly twice as large and is the honest estimate of generalization error.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) 0.04 + 0.09 + 0.3² = **0.22**.\n(b) New error 0.01 + 0.20 + 0.09 = 0.30 > 0.22, so it is **worse**: the variance increase outweighs the bias reduction.\n(c) More training data, bagging or ensembling, regularization, early stopping, feature reduction.\n\n**Q6.**\n```\nimport numpy as np\ndef fit_ridge(X, y, lam):\n    n, d = X.shape\n    I = np.eye(d); I[0, 0] = 0          # do not penalize the intercept column\n    return np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X + lam * I, X.T @ y)\n```\nChoose lam by k-fold cross-validation over a log-spaced grid (for example 1e-4 to 1e2) and pick the value with the lowest validation error; then refit on all training data and evaluate once on the test set. Standardize the features first so that the penalty treats them equally.\n\n# Assignment 2: Classification, Classical Models and Unsupervised Learning (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Logistic regression (14 marks)** Four examples with features (x₁, x₂) = (1, 2), (2, 1), (3, 3), (4, 3) and labels 0, 0, 1, 1. Start from w = (0, 0), b = 0. (a) Give the predicted probabilities and the loss [3]. (b) Compute the gradient with respect to w and b [4]. (c) Perform one update with η = 0.2 and give the new probabilities and loss [5]. (d) A trained model has w = (1, -1), b = 0.5. Find P(y = 1) for x = (2, 1) [2].\n\n**Q2. Evaluation (12 marks)** A fraud detector is tested on 1,000 transactions: TP = 85, FP = 15, FN = 30, TN = 870. (a) Compute accuracy, precision, recall, specificity and F1 [6]. (b) What accuracy does a model that always predicts \"not fraud\" obtain, and why is accuracy a poor metric here? [3] (c) A screening test has sensitivity 0.9 and false-positive rate 0.10 for a condition with prevalence 2 percent. Find P(condition | positive) [3].\n\n**Q3. k-nearest neighbours (8 marks)** Training points (1,1)→0, (1,3)→0, (2,2)→0, (4,4)→1, (5,3)→1, (5,5)→1. Classify the query (3.5, 3) with k = 1 and k = 3 [5]. Explain why features must be standardized for k-NN [3].\n\n**Q4. Decision trees (12 marks)** A node contains 8 positive and 8 negative examples. Split A produces children (7 pos, 1 neg) and (1 pos, 7 neg). Split B produces (4 pos, 8 neg) and (4 pos, 0 neg). (a) Compute the parent entropy and Gini [2]. (b) Compute the information gain and weighted Gini of both splits [6]. (c) Which split is preferred and why? How would you stop the tree from overfitting? [4]\n\n**Q5. k-means (10 marks)** One-dimensional data 1, 2, 4, 11, 12, 15 with initial centroids 2 and 11. (a) Run k-means until convergence, showing assignments and centroids [6]. (b) Compute the final within-cluster sum of squares [2]. (c) Give one weakness of k-means [2].\n\n**Q6. PCA (8 marks)** A dataset has covariance matrix C = [[4, 2], [2, 3]]. (a) Find the eigenvalues [4]. (b) What fraction of the variance does the first principal component explain? [2] (c) Give two uses of PCA [2].\n\n**Q7. Naive Bayes reasoning (6 marks)** Explain the \"naive\" assumption, say when it is violated, and why naive Bayes can still classify well.\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) All logits are 0, so every probability is **0.5** and the loss is ln 2 = **0.6931**.\n(b) p - y = (0.5, 0.5, -0.5, -0.5). ∂J/∂w₁ = (1/4)(0.5×1 + 0.5×2 - 0.5×3 - 0.5×4) = (1/4)(-2) = **-0.5**. ∂J/∂w₂ = (1/4)(0.5×2 + 0.5×1 - 0.5×3 - 0.5×3) = (1/4)(-1.5) = **-0.375**. ∂J/∂b = (1/4)(0) = **0**.\n(c) Step 1: w = (0.1, 0.075), b = 0.\nStep 2: Logits 0.25, 0.2, 0.525, 0.625 give probabilities **0.5622, 0.5683, 0.6283, 0.6514**.\nStep 3: Loss = (1/4)(-ln 0.4378 - ln 0.4317 - ln 0.6283 - ln 0.6514) = **0.6399**, a decrease from 0.6931.\n(d) z = 2 - 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 and σ(1.5) = **0.8176**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: Accuracy = 955/1000 = **0.955**.\nStep 2: Precision = 85/100 = **0.85**.\nStep 3: Recall = 85/115 = **0.739**.\nStep 4: Specificity = 870/885 = **0.983**.\nStep 5: F1 = 2(0.85)(0.739)/(0.85 + 0.739) = **0.791**.\n(b) Predicting \"not fraud\" always gives (870 + 15)/1000 = **0.885** accuracy while catching no fraud at all. Accuracy hides the failure on the rare class; use precision, recall, F1, the precision-recall curve or cost-weighted metrics.\n(c) P = (0.9)(0.02)/[(0.9)(0.02) + (0.10)(0.98)] = 0.018/0.116 = **0.155**. Even a good test gives mostly false alarms when the condition is rare (base-rate effect).\n\n**Q3.** Step 1: Distances from (3.5, 3): to (4,4) 1.118; to (5,3) 1.500; to (2,2) 1.803; to (1,3) 2.500; to (5,5) 2.500; to (1,1) 3.202.\nStep 2: **k = 1**: nearest is (4,4) → class **1**.\nStep 3: **k = 3**: neighbours (4,4), (5,3), (2,2) with labels 1, 1, 0 → majority class **1**.\nStep 4: Distances are dominated by features with large numeric ranges: if x₂ were measured in units 100 times larger it would decide almost every neighbor, so standardize each feature to zero mean and unit variance (fitting the scaler on training data only).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Parent: H = **1.0 bit**, Gini = **0.5**.\n(b) Split A: each child (7,1) or (1,7) has H = 0.5436; weighted H = 0.5436, so **IG = 0.456**; Gini of each child = 1 - (0.875² + 0.125²) = 0.21875, weighted **0.219**. Split B: child (4,8): H = 0.9183, child (4,0): H = 0; weighted H = (12/16)(0.9183) = 0.6887, **IG = 0.311**; Gini (4,8) = 0.4444, (4,0) = 0, weighted **0.333**.\n(c) **Split A** has the larger gain and lower Gini. Limit overfitting by a maximum depth, a minimum number of samples per leaf or split, cost-complexity pruning, or by using an ensemble such as a random forest.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Iteration 1: distances to centroids 2 and 11: points 1, 2, 4 are nearest to 2 (cluster A); 11, 12, 15 nearest to 11 (cluster B). New centroids: A = (1 + 2 + 4)/3 = **2.333**, B = (11 + 12 + 15)/3 = **12.667**. Iteration 2: the assignments are unchanged, so the algorithm has **converged**.\n(b) A: (1.333² + 0.333² + 1.667²) = 4.667; B: (1.667² + 0.667² + 2.333²) = 8.667. Total **13.33**.\n(c) Sensitive to initialization (local optima), requires k to be chosen in advance, assumes roughly spherical equal-size clusters, and is affected by outliers.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) det(C - λI) = (4 - λ)(3 - λ) - 4 = λ² - 7λ + 8 = 0, so λ = (7 ± √17)/2 = **5.562 and 1.438**.\n(b) 5.562/7 = **0.795** (79.5 percent). The first component direction is (0.788, 0.615).\n(c) Visualization in 2D or 3D, compression and noise reduction, decorrelating features before another model, speeding up training.\n\n**Q7.** The \"naive\" assumption is that features are conditionally independent given the class, so P(x | y) = Π P(x_j | y). It is violated whenever features are correlated (for example the words \"New\" and \"York\"). The probabilities then become over-confident, but classification only needs the ranking of the classes, which often stays correct; the model has few parameters, trains in one pass and needs little data.\n\n# Assignment 3: Neural Networks, Optimization and Deployment (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Backpropagation of one neuron (12 marks)** A neuron has weights w = (0.5, 0.5), bias b = 0.5 and ReLU activation. Loss L = ½(a - t)² with input x = (2, 1) and target t = 3. (a) Compute z, a and L [3]. (b) Compute ∂L/∂w and ∂L/∂b [4]. (c) Apply one gradient step with η = 0.1 and compute the new loss [5].\n\n**Q2. Softmax and cross-entropy (10 marks)** Logits z = (1, 2, 3). (a) Compute the softmax probabilities [3]. (b) Compute the cross-entropy loss if the true class is the third (index 2) and if it is the first [3]. (c) Give the gradient of the loss with respect to z for each case [2]. (d) What loss would you expect at initialization for a 4-class problem with uniform predictions? [2]\n\n**Q3. Optimizers (12 marks)** Minimize f(w) = w² from w = 2 (gradient 2w). (a) Perform three steps of SGD with momentum (v ← 0.5v + g, w ← w - 0.1 v) [5]. (b) Perform three steps of Adam (η = 0.1, β₁ = 0.9, β₂ = 0.999, ε = 1e-8) and comment on the first step [5]. (c) When is Adam preferred over plain SGD? [2]\n\n**Q4. Convolutional layers (12 marks)** (a) Compute the output size for: input 224, kernel 7, padding 3, stride 2; input 56, kernel 3, padding 1, stride 1; input 56, kernel 3, padding 0, stride 2; input 64, kernel 5, padding 2, stride 1 [4]. (b) How many parameters has a 3×3 convolution from 64 to 128 channels (with bias), a 7×7 convolution from 3 to 64 channels, and a dense layer from 9,216 to 4,096 units? [4] (c) Explain why convolutions suit images better than dense layers [4].\n\n**Q5. Network capacity (8 marks)** (a) Count the parameters of an MLP with layer sizes 100-50-10 [2]. (b) Show that two linear layers without a non-linearity collapse to one layer, using W₁ = [[1, 2], [0, 1]] and W₂ = [3, 1] [3]. (c) Why is ReLU usually preferred to the sigmoid in hidden layers? [3]\n\n**Q6. Normalization and regularization (8 marks)** (a) Apply batch normalization (ε = 1e-5, γ = 1, β = 0) to the activations 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 [4]. (b) Describe how dropout works in training and at test time [2]. (c) What is early stopping? [2]\n\n**Q7. Deployment (8 marks)** A model has 25 million parameters stored in float32. (a) Give its size in MB and the size after int8 quantization [2]. (b) Name three things to monitor after deployment and the risk of each [3]. (c) Explain data drift versus concept drift [3].\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) z = 0.5×2 + 0.5×1 + 0.5 = 2.0; since z > 0, a = **2.0**; L = ½(2 - 3)² = **0.5**.\n(b) ∂L/∂a = a - t = -1; the ReLU derivative is 1 (z > 0), so ∂L/∂z = -1. Then ∂L/∂w = -1 × x = **(-2, -1)** and ∂L/∂b = **-1**.\n(c) w = (0.5 + 0.2, 0.5 + 0.1) = **(0.7, 0.6)**, b = **0.6**. New z = 1.4 + 0.6 + 0.6 = 2.6, a = 2.6, L = ½(0.4)² = **0.08**: the loss fell from 0.5 to 0.08.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) e¹ = 2.718, e² = 7.389, e³ = 20.086; sum 30.193. p = **(0.0900, 0.2447, 0.6652)**.\n(b) True class 2: -ln 0.6652 = **0.4076**. True class 0: -ln 0.0900 = **2.4076**.\n(c) Gradient = p - one-hot: true class 2 → **(0.090, 0.245, -0.335)**; true class 0 → **(-0.910, 0.245, 0.665)**.\n(d) Uniform predictions give p = 1/4 and loss ln 4 = **1.386**. A very different starting loss signals a bug or bad initialization.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: g = 4, v = 4, w = 2 - 0.4 = **1.6**. Step 2: g = 3.2, v = 0.5×4 + 3.2 = 5.2, w = 1.6 - 0.52 = **1.08**. Step 3: g = 2.16, v = 2.6 + 2.16 = 4.76, w = 1.08 - 0.476 = **0.604**.\n(b) Step 1: g = 4; m = 0.4, v = 0.016; m̂ = 4, v̂ = 16; step = 0.1 × 4/(4 + 1e-8) ≈ 0.1, so **w = 1.9**. Step 2: g = 3.8, m̂ = 3.895, v̂ = 15.22, step ≈ 0.0999, **w = 1.8002**. Step 3: **w = 1.7006**. The first step is almost exactly η in magnitude whatever the gradient scale: Adam normalizes by the gradient magnitude, so each step is about η per parameter.\n(c) Adam adapts per-parameter step sizes, works well with sparse or noisy gradients and needs little tuning, so it is a strong default for transformers and many deep nets. Well-tuned SGD with momentum often generalizes slightly better on image tasks.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) ⌊(224 + 6 - 7)/2⌋ + 1 = **112**; ⌊(56 + 2 - 3)/1⌋ + 1 = **56**; ⌊(56 - 3)/2⌋ + 1 = **27**; ⌊(64 + 4 - 5)/1⌋ + 1 = **64**.\n(b) 3×3×64×128 + 128 = **73,856**; 7×7×3×64 + 64 = **9,472**; 9,216×4,096 + 4,096 = **37,752,832**.\n(c) Weight sharing: the same small kernel is applied at every position, so the parameter count is tiny and independent of image size; locality: nearby pixels are correlated and form edges and textures; translation equivariance: a feature is detected wherever it appears; and stacking layers builds a hierarchy from edges to parts to objects.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) (100×50 + 50) + (50×10 + 10) = 5,050 + 510 = **5,560**.\n(b) W₂W₁ = [3, 1] [[1, 2], [0, 1]] = [3×1 + 1×0, 3×2 + 1×1] = **[3, 7]**, a single linear layer, so depth adds nothing without non-linearity.\n(c) The sigmoid saturates: its derivative is at most 0.25 and near 0 for large |z|, causing vanishing gradients in deep networks; its output is not zero-centered. ReLU has gradient 1 for z > 0, is cheap and gives sparse activations (with the risk of dead units, mitigated by leaky ReLU).\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Mean 5, variance (16 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 16)/5 = 8, so the normalized values are (x - 5)/√(8.00001) = **-1.414, -0.707, 0, 0.707, 1.414**.\n(b) In training each unit is zeroed with probability p and the remaining outputs are scaled by 1/(1 - p) (inverted dropout), which prevents co-adaptation; at test time all units are used with no scaling.\n(c) Monitor validation loss during training and stop (keeping the best checkpoint) when it stops improving for a set number of epochs, to prevent overfitting.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) 25×10⁶ × 4 bytes = **100 MB**; int8 uses 1 byte each = **25 MB** (4× smaller, usually with small accuracy loss).\n(b) Examples: input data distribution (drift makes predictions unreliable); prediction distribution and confidence (silent degradation); latency and throughput (service-level failures); accuracy against delayed labels; fairness across subgroups.\n(c) **Data drift**: the input distribution P(x) changes (a new camera, a new user population) while the relationship may still hold. **Concept drift**: the relationship P(y | x) itself changes (user tastes shift, fraud tactics evolve). Both require monitoring and retraining.\n","exam":"# Machine Learning and AI: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\nUseful values: σ(0) = 0.5, e¹ = 2.718, e² = 7.389, e^0.5 = 1.649, e^-1 = 0.368, ln 2 = 0.693, ln 4 = 1.386.\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Compared with a simple model, a very flexible model typically has\nA) higher bias and higher variance  B) lower bias and higher variance  C) higher bias and lower variance  D) lower bias and lower variance\n\n**2.** The main purpose of k-fold cross-validation is to\nA) increase the training set  B) remove outliers  C) estimate generalization performance and choose hyperparameters  D) speed up training\n\n**3.** Which of these is an example of data leakage?\nA) fitting a scaler on the whole dataset before splitting  B) fitting the scaler on training data only  C) shuffling the training set  D) using a held-out test set\n\n**4.** Standardizing features helps gradient descent because it\nA) removes all noise  B) reduces the number of parameters  C) guarantees zero training error  D) makes the loss surface better conditioned so one learning rate suits all directions\n\n**5.** Compared with ridge, the lasso penalty\nA) can set weights exactly to zero  B) never shrinks weights  C) is always faster to compute  D) cannot be used with regression\n\n**6.** For softmax with cross-entropy, the gradient of the loss with respect to the logits is\nA) y - z  B) p²  C) p - y (probabilities minus the one-hot label)  D) log p\n\n**7.** An ROC AUC of 0.5 means the classifier\nA) is perfect  B) ranks no better than chance  C) is always wrong  D) has 50 percent precision\n\n**8.** The Gini impurity of a pure node is\nA) 0  B) 0.5  C) 1  D) undefined\n\n**9.** A random forest decorrelates its trees by\nA) pruning all trees  B) using one deep tree  C) training on test data  D) bootstrap sampling and random feature subsets at each split\n\n**10.** Gradient boosting builds trees\nA) independently and averages them  B) at random  C) sequentially, each fitting the errors (negative gradient) of the ensemble so far  D) only on the test set\n\n**11.** k-means++ initialization is intended to\nA) choose k automatically  B) spread out the initial centroids to avoid poor local optima  C) guarantee the global optimum  D) remove outliers\n\n**12.** When using PCA before a classifier, the PCA should be fitted on\nA) the training data only  B) the test data  C) the whole dataset  D) the labels\n\n**13.** A \"dead\" ReLU unit is one that\nA) has weights equal to one  B) has a large positive output  C) is used in the output layer  D) outputs zero for essentially all inputs, so it receives no gradient\n\n**14.** He initialization is designed for\nA) sigmoid units  B) linear regression  C) ReLU units  D) k-means\n\n**15.** Stacking many sigmoid layers causes vanishing gradients mainly because\nA) the weights are too large  B) the sigmoid derivative is at most 0.25, so products shrink exponentially  C) there is no bias  D) the loss is not differentiable\n\n**16.** The Adam optimizer combines\nA) momentum-like first moments with per-parameter scaling by second moments  B) random restarts with pruning  C) bagging with boosting  D) PCA with SGD\n\n**17.** Batch normalization normalizes each feature\nA) over the whole training set once  B) across the layers  C) over the labels  D) over the current mini-batch, followed by a learned scale and shift\n\n**18.** At test time, dropout\nA) randomly zeroes units again  B) doubles the weights  C) uses all units (with the training-time scaling convention already accounted for)  D) is replaced by L1\n\n**19.** Scaled dot-product attention computes\nA) argmax(QKᵀ) V  B) softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k) V  C) QKᵀ + V  D) ReLU(QV)\n\n**20.** When the relationship P(y | x) changes over time (for example fraud tactics evolve) this is called\nA) concept drift  B) covariate invariance  C) overfitting  D) regularization\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Error decomposition (5 marks)** A model has squared bias 0.09, variance 0.16 and the data have noise standard deviation 0.2. (a) Compute the expected squared error [2]. (b) The variance is cut by bagging to 0.05 at the cost of 0.01 extra bias². Is the ensemble better? [3]\n\n**B2. Base rate (5 marks)** A test for a rare condition has sensitivity 0.95 and false-positive rate 0.05; prevalence is 2 percent. Find P(condition | positive) and interpret it.\n\n**B3. Information gain (5 marks)** A node has 12 positive and 4 negative examples. A split sends (10 pos, 1 neg) to the left child and (2 pos, 3 neg) to the right. Compute the entropy of the parent and children and the information gain.\n\n**B4. AUC (5 marks)** Labels (1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0) and scores (0.8, 0.6, 0.55, 0.4, 0.35, 0.1). Compute the AUC by counting positive-negative pairs, and say what it means.\n\n**B5. k-means step (5 marks)** Points (0,0), (0,2), (4,0), (4,2), (5,1) with initial centroids (0,0) and (4,0). Perform one assignment and update step.\n\n**B6. Softmax (5 marks)** Logits z = (2, 0.5, -1). Compute the probabilities and the cross-entropy loss when the true class is 0 and when it is 1.\n\n**B7. Learning rate (5 marks)** Minimize f(w) = w² from w = 3 by gradient descent. Give the first three iterates for η = 0.25. What happens for η = 1 and for η = 1.5?\n\n**B8. A small CNN (5 marks)** A 28×28 grayscale image passes through a 5×5 convolution (1 → 8 channels, no padding, stride 1), a 2×2 max pool with stride 2, then flatten and a dense layer to 10 outputs. Give the shape after each stage and the parameter count of the convolution and the dense layer.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 40 marks)\n\n**C1. Regression (10 marks)** Data (x, y): (1, 1.2), (2, 1.9), (3, 3.2), (4, 3.8), (5, 5.1), (6, 5.9). (a) Write XᵀX and Xᵀy for ŷ = b + w x [2]. (b) Find b and w [3]. (c) Compute the residuals, MSE and R² [3]. (d) Predict y at x = 7 and compute the standard error of the slope and its t-statistic, using s² = SS_res/(n - 2) [2].\n\n**C2. Logistic regression and decision costs (10 marks)** (a) A one-feature logistic model is trained on x = (-2, -1, 1, 2) with labels (0, 1, 0, 1), starting from w = b = 0. Find the loss and the gradient [3]. (b) Perform one update with η = 1 and compute the new probabilities and loss [3]. (c) A screening system has three operating thresholds on 1,000 cases containing 50 true positives. At the low threshold TP = 40 and FP = 60; at the middle TP = 30 and FP = 20; at the high TP = 20 and FP = 5. A missed positive costs 100 and a false alarm costs 5. Compute the total cost and the precision and recall at each threshold and recommend one [4].\n\n**C3. Backpropagation (10 marks)** A network has two inputs, two tanh hidden units and a linear output with squared loss L = ½(o - t)². W₁ = [[0.2, -0.1], [0.4, 0.3]], b₁ = (0, 0.1), W₂ = (1, -1), b₂ = 0.2. The input is x = (1, 2) and the target t = 0.5. (a) Perform the forward pass and compute the loss [3]. (b) Compute all gradients ∂L/∂W₂, ∂L/∂b₂, ∂L/∂W₁, ∂L/∂b₁ [5]. (c) Apply one step with η = 0.1 and give the new output and loss [2].\n\n**C4. PCA and compression (10 marks)** Data (1,1), (2,3), (3,2), (4,4). (a) Compute the mean and the sample covariance matrix [3]. (b) Find the eigenvalues and the principal directions [3]. (c) Give the proportion of variance explained by the first component and the 1D scores of the four points [2]. (d) What is the total squared reconstruction error if only the first component is kept? [2]\n\n**C5. System design and deployment (10 marks)** You must deploy a 7-million-parameter vision model on a battery-powered camera. (a) Give its float32 and int8 sizes [2]. (b) Training uses 50,000 images, batch size 128 and 30 epochs. How many iterations per epoch and in total? [2] (c) Describe a sound train/validation/test strategy if the data are video frames from 20 different cameras and the model will be deployed on new cameras [3]. (d) Outline a monitoring plan after deployment, including how you detect drift and what triggers retraining [3].\n\n## Answer key and mark scheme\n\n### Section A\n1. B 2. C 3. A 4. D 5. A 6. C 7. B 8. A 9. D 10. C 11. B 12. A 13. D 14. C 15. B 16. A 17. D 18. C 19. B 20. A\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** (a) 0.09 + 0.16 + 0.2² = 0.09 + 0.16 + 0.04 = **0.29** [2]. (b) New error = 0.10 + 0.05 + 0.04 = **0.19** < 0.29, so the ensemble is **better**: the variance reduction outweighs the small bias increase [3].\n\n**B2.** P = (0.95)(0.02)/[(0.95)(0.02) + (0.05)(0.98)] = 0.019/(0.019 + 0.049) = **0.279** [3]. Only about 28 percent of positives are true cases because the condition is rare; a positive result should lead to a confirmatory test, and the base rate must be considered when judging screening accuracy [2].\n\n**B3.** Parent: p = 12/16 = 0.75, H = -(0.75 log₂ 0.75 + 0.25 log₂ 0.25) = **0.811** [1]. Left (10, 1): H = **0.439**; right (2, 3): H = **0.971** [2]. Weighted child entropy = (11/16)(0.439) + (5/16)(0.971) = 0.302 + 0.303 = 0.605. **Information gain = 0.811 - 0.605 = 0.206** [2].\n\n**B4.** Positives score 0.8, 0.55, 0.35; negatives 0.6, 0.4, 0.1. Of 9 pairs: 0.8 beats all three negatives (3); 0.55 beats 0.4 and 0.1 but not 0.6 (2); 0.35 beats 0.1 only (1). Correct pairs = 6, **AUC = 6/9 = 0.667** [4]. It is the probability that a random positive receives a higher score than a random negative [1].\n\n**B5.** Squared distances to (0,0) and (4,0): (0,0): 0, 16 → A; (0,2): 4, 20 → A; (4,0): 16, 0 → B; (4,2): 20, 4 → B; (5,1): 26, 2 → B [3]. New centroids: A = (0, 1); B = ((4 + 4 + 5)/3, (0 + 2 + 1)/3) = **(4.333, 1)** [2].\n\n**B6.** e² = 7.389, e^0.5 = 1.649, e^-1 = 0.368; sum = 9.406. p = **(0.7856, 0.1753, 0.0391)** [3]. Loss for true class 0: -ln 0.7856 = **0.241**; for true class 1: -ln 0.1753 = **1.741** [2].\n\n**B7.** Update w ← w(1 - 2η). For η = 0.25: **1.5, 0.75, 0.375** (halving each step: convergent) [3]. For η = 1: w ← -w, so 3 → -3 → 3 → -3: it **oscillates forever without converging**. For η = 1.5: w ← -2w, giving -6, 12, -24: it **diverges** [2].\n\n**B8.** Input 28×28×1 → conv: **24×24×8**, parameters 5×5×1×8 + 8 = **208** [2]. Pool 2×2 stride 2 → **12×12×8** [1]. Flatten → **1,152** values. Dense to 10: 1,152 × 10 + 10 = **11,530** parameters [2].\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1.**\n(a) XᵀX = [[6, 21], [21, 91]], Xᵀy = [21.1, 90.7] [2].\n(b) x̄ = 3.5, ȳ = 3.5167; S_xx = 17.5 and S_xy = 16.85. **w = 16.85/17.5 = 0.963**, **b = 3.5167 - 0.9629 × 3.5 = 0.147** [3].\n(c) Fitted: 1.110, 2.073, 3.036, 3.999, 4.961, 5.924. Residuals: **0.090, -0.172, 0.165, -0.198, 0.139, -0.024** (sum 0). SS_res = 0.1242, **MSE = 0.0207**. SS_tot = Σ(y - 3.5167)² = 16.348; **R² = 1 - 0.1242/16.348 = 0.992** [3].\n(d) ŷ(7) = 0.147 + 6.740 = **6.887**. s² = 0.1242/4 = 0.03105; SE(w) = √(0.03105/17.5) = **0.0421**; t = 0.963/0.0421 = **22.9**, so the slope is highly significant [2].\n\n**C2.**\n(a) At w = b = 0 all probabilities are 0.5, loss = **ln 2 = 0.693** (also check: -ln 0.5 for each example). Gradient: ∂J/∂w = (1/4) Σ (p - y) x = (1/4)[(0.5)(-2) + (-0.5)(-1) + (0.5)(1) + (-0.5)(2)] = (1/4)(-1 + 0.5 + 0.5 - 1) = **-0.25**; ∂J/∂b = (1/4)(0.5 - 0.5 + 0.5 - 0.5) = **0** [3].\n(b) w = 0, b = 0 → w = 0 + 1 × 0.25 = **0.25**, b = 0. Probabilities σ(0.25x): **0.3775, 0.4378, 0.5622, 0.6225**. Loss = (1/4)[-ln(1 - 0.3775) - ln 0.4378 - ln(1 - 0.5622) - ln 0.6225] = (1/4)(0.474 + 0.826 + 0.826 + 0.474) = **0.650** [3].\n(c) Cost = 100 FN + 5 FP. Low: FN = 10, FP = 60 → 1000 + 300 = **1300**; precision 40/100 = 0.40, recall 40/50 = 0.80. Middle: FN = 20, FP = 20 → 2000 + 100 = **2100**; precision 0.60, recall 0.60. High: FN = 30, FP = 5 → 3000 + 25 = **3025**; precision 0.80, recall 0.40. **Recommend the low threshold** (lowest cost 1300): missing a positive is 20 times more costly than a false alarm, so high recall is worth the extra false alarms [4].\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Hidden pre-activations: unit 1: 0.2(1) - 0.1(2) + 0 = **0**; unit 2: 0.4(1) + 0.3(2) + 0.1 = **1.1**. Hidden outputs h = (tanh 0, tanh 1.1) = **(0, 0.8005)**. Output o = 1(0) - 1(0.8005) + 0.2 = **-0.6005**. Loss = ½(-0.6005 - 0.5)² = **0.6055** [3].\n(b) δ_o = o - t = **-1.1005**. ∂L/∂W₂ = δ_o h = **(0, -0.8809)**; ∂L/∂b₂ = **-1.1005**. Hidden deltas: δ_h = δ_o W₂ ⊙ (1 - h²) = (-1.1005 × 1 × 1, -1.1005 × -1 × (1 - 0.6408)) = **(-1.1005, 0.3953)**. ∂L/∂W₁ = δ_h xᵀ = **[[-1.1005, -2.2010], [0.3953, 0.7906]]**; ∂L/∂b₁ = **(-1.1005, 0.3953)** [5].\n(c) With η = 0.1 the new parameters give o = **0.2524** and loss **0.0307**: a large decrease from 0.6055 [2].\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Mean = (2.5, 2.5). Centered points: (-1.5, -1.5), (-0.5, 0.5), (0.5, -0.5), (1.5, 1.5). Covariance (divide by n - 1 = 3): variances 5/3 = **1.667**, covariance 4/3 = **1.333**: C = [[1.667, 1.333], [1.333, 1.667]] [3].\n(b) Eigenvalues 1.667 ± 1.333 = **3.0 and 0.333**; directions (1, 1)/√2 and (-1, 1)/√2 [3].\n(c) Explained by the first component: 3/3.333 = **0.90** (90 percent). Scores: (-1.5)(0.7071) × 2 = **-2.121, 0, 0, 2.121** [2].\n(d) The discarded component has scores (0, 0.707, -0.707, 0), whose squares sum to **1.0** (equal to λ₂ × (n - 1) = 0.333 × 3) [2].\n\n**C5.**\n(a) float32: 7×10⁶ × 4 bytes = **28 MB**; int8: **7 MB** (4× smaller; quantization-aware training reduces any accuracy loss) [2].\n(b) 50,000/128 = 390.6, so **391 iterations per epoch** (the last batch is smaller), and 30 × 391 = **11,730 iterations** in total [2].\n(c) Frames from the same video are highly correlated, so a random frame split leaks information. Split **by camera** (for example 14 cameras for training, 3 for validation, 3 for testing; or cross-validate leaving cameras out) so that the test cameras mimic new, unseen deployment conditions; keep the test cameras untouched until the end; fit all normalization on the training cameras only [3].\n(d) Log input statistics (brightness, resolution, class frequencies) and compare them with the training distribution (population stability index, KS test) to detect data drift; track the prediction distribution and confidence; collect a sample of labelled field data to measure accuracy and per-camera or per-subgroup error rates; monitor latency, power and memory. Trigger an alert and retrain or fine-tune when drift or measured accuracy crosses a preset threshold, validate the new model on held-out cameras, then roll out gradually with the ability to roll back [3].\n","notes":"# Machine Learning and AI: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nPrerequisites: linear algebra, multivariable calculus (gradients), probability and statistics, and Python with NumPy. Every worked number in these notes was computed with NumPy, scikit-learn or PyTorch. Re-derive each example by hand once, then reproduce it in code.\n\n## Chapter 1. What Machine Learning Is\n\n### 1.1 The learning problem\nA model is a function f_θ that maps an input x to a prediction ŷ. **Learning** means choosing the parameters θ so that the predictions are good on data the model has not seen. We are given a training set of N examples. The standard setting splits into three families:\n- **Supervised learning**: each example has a label y. *Regression* predicts a number, *classification* predicts a class.\n- **Unsupervised learning**: no labels; find structure (clustering, dimensionality reduction, density estimation).\n- **Reinforcement learning**: an agent acts in an environment and learns from rewards.\n\n### 1.2 Loss, risk and generalization\nA **loss function** L(ŷ, y) scores one prediction. The **empirical risk** is the average loss on the training set, and training minimizes it. What we actually care about is the **expected risk** on new data from the same distribution. The gap between the two is the **generalization gap**. A model with tiny training loss and large test loss has **overfit**; a model with large loss on both has **underfit**.\n\n### 1.3 Data splits\n- **Training set**: used to fit parameters.\n- **Validation set**: used to choose hyperparameters (model size, learning rate, regularization strength).\n- **Test set**: used once, at the end, to report performance. If you tune on the test set it stops being an honest estimate.\n\nTypical splits are 60/20/20 or 70/15/15. With small data use **k-fold cross-validation**: split the data into k parts, train on k - 1, validate on the remaining part, rotate, and average. For n = 100 and k = 5, each fold trains on 80 examples and validates on 20.\n\n**Data leakage** is the most common reason a model looks excellent in the notebook and fails in production. Fit scalers, imputers and feature selectors on the training data only; for time series always split by time, never randomly.\n\n### 1.4 The machine learning workflow\n1. Define the problem and the metric the business or system cares about.\n2. Collect and inspect the data; look for missing values, outliers, label noise and class imbalance.\n3. Build a trivial baseline (predict the mean, or the majority class). Any model must beat it.\n4. Engineer features, train models, tune on validation data.\n5. Evaluate once on the test set, analyse errors, then deploy and monitor.\n\n## Chapter 2. Linear Regression\n\n### 2.1 Model and least squares\nThe model is ŷ = w·x + b. Stack the examples into a design matrix X (with a column of ones for the intercept) and minimize the mean squared error J(w) = (1/N) ||Xw - y||². Setting the gradient to zero gives the **normal equations**:\n\n```\n(Xᵀ X) w = Xᵀ y\n```\n\n**Worked example.** Data (x, y) = (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 5), (4, 4). Then XᵀX = [[4, 10], [10, 30]] and Xᵀy = [14, 39]. Solving gives intercept b = 1.5 and slope w = 0.8. The residuals are -0.3, -0.1, 1.1, -0.7, giving MSE = 0.45 and R² = 1 - SS_res/SS_tot = 0.64.\n\n### 2.2 Gradient descent\nFor large problems we iterate: w ← w - η ∇J(w), where ∇J = (2/N) Xᵀ(Xw - y) and η is the **learning rate**.\n\n**Worked example (same data, η = 0.1, start at b = w = 0).** The first gradient is [-7, -19.5], giving (b, w) = (0.7, 1.95). The second gradient is [4.15, 13.25], giving (0.285, 0.625). The iterates oscillate because the unscaled feature x has large values. **Standardizing features** (subtract mean, divide by standard deviation) makes the loss surface rounder and gradient descent faster and more stable.\n\nOn f(w) = w² starting from w = 1: with η = 0.1 the iterates are 0.8, 0.64, 0.512 (converging); with η = 1.1 they are -1.2, 1.44, -1.728 (diverging). A learning rate that is too large diverges; too small is slow.\n\nVariants: **batch** GD uses all N examples per step; **stochastic** GD uses one; **mini-batch** SGD (32 to 512 examples) is the practical default because it is noisy enough to escape shallow traps yet vectorizes well.\n\n### 2.3 Polynomial features and overfitting\nFeeding x, x², x³, ... to a linear model gives a flexible curve. With 10 noisy samples of sin(2πx) (noise standard deviation 0.3) the experiment below, averaged over 200 resamples, shows the trade-off.\n\n- Degree 1 (a straight line): bias² 0.225, variance 0.016. It underfits.\n- Degree 3: bias² 0.006, variance 0.030. Close to the sweet spot.\n- Degree 9: bias² 0.002, variance 0.620. It passes through every training point and overfits: tiny bias, huge variance.\n\n## Chapter 3. Bias, Variance and Regularization\n\n### 3.1 Bias-variance decomposition\nFor squared error, the expected test error at a point splits as\n\n```\nE[(y - ŷ)²] = Bias² + Variance + Noise\n```\n\n*Bias* is the error from wrong assumptions (a line cannot fit a sine). *Variance* is the sensitivity to the particular training sample. *Noise* is irreducible. For example, bias = 0.5, variance = 0.3 and noise standard deviation 0.2 give expected error 0.25 + 0.3 + 0.04 = 0.59. Simple models have high bias and low variance; complex models the reverse. The goal is the sweet spot, found by validation.\n\n### 3.2 Ridge (L2) regularization\nAdd a penalty: J(w) = MSE + λ ||w||². The closed form is w = (XᵀX + λI)⁻¹ Xᵀy (do not penalize the intercept). Ridge shrinks weights toward zero and stabilizes ill-conditioned problems.\n\n**Worked example.** For the data of section 2.1 the centered slope is S_xy / (S_xx + λ) with S_xy = 4 and S_xx = 5. The slope is 0.8 for λ = 0, 0.667 for λ = 1 and 0.4 for λ = 5, and the intercept rises correspondingly (1.5, 1.833, 2.5) so the line still passes through the means.\n\n### 3.3 Lasso (L1) regularization\nThe penalty λ Σ|w_j| drives some weights to exactly zero, giving **sparse** models and implicit feature selection. For a single weight the solution is the **soft threshold**: shrink by λ and clip at zero. With λ = 0.5: 0.8 becomes 0.3, 0.3 becomes 0, and -1.2 becomes -0.7. Geometrically the L1 ball has corners on the axes, where the loss contours tend to touch it.\n\n### 3.4 Other ways to control variance\nMore data, early stopping, dropout (for networks), data augmentation, ensembling (section 6) and simpler models. Choose λ by cross-validation on a log grid (1e-4 ... 1e2).\n\n## Chapter 4. Classification\n\n### 4.1 Logistic regression\nFor two classes, model P(y = 1 | x) = σ(w·x + b) with the sigmoid σ(z) = 1/(1 + e^-z). Train with the **binary cross-entropy** (log loss):\n\n```\nJ = -(1/N) Σ [ y log p + (1 - y) log(1 - p) ]\n```\n\nIts gradient is neat: ∂J/∂w = (1/N) Σ (p - y) x and ∂J/∂b = (1/N) Σ (p - y). The loss is convex, so gradient descent reaches the global optimum.\n\n**Worked example.** x = 1, 2, 3, 4 with labels 0, 0, 1, 1, starting from w = b = 0. All predictions are 0.5 and the loss is ln 2 = 0.6931. The gradients are ∂J/∂w = -0.5 and ∂J/∂b = 0. With η = 0.5 the update gives w = 0.25, b = 0, new probabilities 0.562, 0.622, 0.679, 0.731 and loss 0.6250.\n\nThe decision boundary w·x + b = 0 is a hyperplane: logistic regression is a *linear* classifier. Why cross-entropy rather than MSE? For predictions (0.9, 0.2, 0.6) and labels (1, 0, 1) the log loss is 0.280 and the MSE 0.070, but the cross-entropy gradient does not vanish when the model is confidently wrong, whereas MSE with a sigmoid saturates.\n\n### 4.2 Softmax and multiclass\nFor K classes, logits z produce probabilities softmax(z)_k = e^{z_k} / Σ e^{z_j}, trained with cross-entropy -log p_true. The gradient with respect to the logits is simply p - y (one-hot). **Worked example.** z = (2.0, 1.0, 0.1) gives p = (0.659, 0.242, 0.099). If the true class is 0, the loss is -ln 0.659 = 0.417 and the gradient is (-0.341, 0.242, 0.099). For a two-example batch with logits (2, 1, 0.1) → class 0 and (0.5, 2.5, 0.3) → class 1, the losses are 0.417 and 0.220, mean 0.3185.\n\n### 4.3 Evaluation metrics\nAccuracy misleads on imbalanced data (99 percent negatives means a model that always says \"negative\" scores 99 percent). Use the confusion matrix:\n\n```\n              predicted +    predicted -\nactual +         TP             FN\nactual -         FP             TN\n```\n\n- Precision = TP/(TP + FP): of the alarms, how many are real.\n- Recall (sensitivity, TPR) = TP/(TP + FN): of the real positives, how many are caught.\n- Specificity = TN/(TN + FP); false-positive rate = FP/(FP + TN).\n- F1 = 2PR/(P + R), the harmonic mean.\n\n**Worked example.** TP = 40, FP = 10, FN = 20, TN = 130 (N = 200). Accuracy = 170/200 = 0.85, precision = 0.80, recall = 0.667, F1 = 0.727, specificity = 0.929, FPR = 0.071.\n\nThreshold choice trades precision against recall. The **ROC curve** plots TPR against FPR as the threshold varies; the **AUC** is the probability that a random positive is scored above a random negative (0.5 = chance, 1 = perfect). For scores (0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.6, 0.55, 0.4, 0.35, 0.2) with labels (1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0), AUC = 0.75. At threshold 0.5 we get TP = 3, FP = 2, FN = 1, TN = 2 (precision 0.6, recall 0.75); at 0.65, TP = 2, FP = 1, FN = 2, TN = 3 (precision 0.667, recall 0.5). Use precision-recall curves when positives are rare.\n\n## Chapter 5. Classical Models\n\n### 5.1 k-nearest neighbours\nPredict by majority vote of the k closest training points. No training, but slow prediction and sensitive to feature scale (standardize!). With training points (1,1)→0, (2,1)→0, (4,3)→1, (5,4)→1, (3,3)→0 and query (3.5, 3), the distances are 3.20, 2.50, 0.50, 1.80, 0.50. The 3 nearest are (4,3), (3,3) and (5,4): labels 1, 0, 1, so the vote is class 1. Small k means high variance, large k high bias.\n\n### 5.2 Naive Bayes\nAssume features are independent given the class: P(y | x) ∝ P(y) Π P(x_j | y). With Laplace smoothing, a word count is (count + 1)/(total + 2) for a binary feature. **Worked example (spam).** 10 emails: 4 spam, 6 ham. \"free\" appears in 3 of the 4 spam and 1 of the 6 ham; \"meeting\" in 0 of the 4 spam and 3 of the 6 ham. Smoothed: P(free | spam) = 4/6, P(free | ham) = 2/8, P(meeting | spam) = 1/6, P(meeting | ham) = 4/8. For an email containing both words, score(spam) = 0.4 × 0.667 × 0.1667 = 0.0444 and score(ham) = 0.6 × 0.25 × 0.5 = 0.075. The posterior for spam is 0.0444/0.1194 = 0.372, so it is classified ham.\n\n### 5.3 Decision trees\nA tree splits the data by feature thresholds. Choose splits that reduce impurity. For class proportions p_k: entropy H = -Σ p_k log₂ p_k and Gini G = 1 - Σ p_k². **Worked example.** A node has 9 positives and 5 negatives: H = 0.940 and G = 0.459. A split sends (6 pos, 2 neg) left (H = 0.811, G = 0.375) and (3, 3) right (H = 1.0, G = 0.5). Information gain = 0.940 - (8/14 × 0.811 + 6/14 × 1.0) = 0.048; the weighted Gini after the split is 0.429. Trees overfit unless limited by depth, minimum samples per leaf or pruning.\n\n### 5.4 Support vector machines\nA linear SVM finds the hyperplane with the largest **margin**. With w·x + b = ±1 on the support vectors the margin width is 2/||w||. Example: w = (0.5, 0.5), b = -1 gives +1 at (2,2) and -1 at (0,0), margin 2/0.707 = 2.83. The soft-margin parameter C trades margin against errors. **Kernels** (RBF, polynomial) compute inner products in a high-dimensional space without forming it, giving non-linear boundaries.\n\n## Chapter 6. Ensembles\n\n- **Bagging**: train many models on bootstrap resamples and average. Averaging n independent predictors of variance σ² gives variance σ²/n (σ² = 4, n = 25 gives 0.16); in practice the models are correlated so the gain is smaller. A **random forest** adds random feature subsets to decorrelate trees.\n- **Boosting**: train models sequentially, each correcting the previous errors (AdaBoost, gradient boosting, XGBoost, LightGBM). Gradient-boosted trees are usually the strongest method on tabular data.\n- **Stacking**: a meta-model combines base-model predictions.\n\n## Chapter 7. Unsupervised Learning\n\n### 7.1 k-means\nAlternate: assign each point to the nearest centroid, then move each centroid to its cluster mean. It minimizes the within-cluster sum of squares and converges to a local optimum, so run it several times (k-means++ initialization). **Worked example.** Points (1,1), (1.5,2), (3,5), (5,7), (3.5,5), (4.5,5), (3.5,4.5), initial centroids (1,1) and (5,7). Iteration 1 assigns the first two points to cluster A and the other five to cluster B; the new centroids are (1.25, 1.5) and (3.9, 5.3). A second iteration changes no assignments, so it has converged. Choose k with the elbow of the inertia curve or the silhouette score.\n\n### 7.2 Principal component analysis\nPCA finds orthogonal directions of maximal variance: eigenvectors of the covariance matrix. Steps: center the data, compute the covariance, take eigenvalues in decreasing order, project onto the top components. **Worked example.** Data (2,0), (0,2), (3,1), (1,3), (4,4), mean (2,2), covariance [[2.5, 0.5], [0.5, 2.5]]. The eigenvalues are 3 (direction (1,1)/√2) and 2 (direction (-1,1)/√2), so the first component explains 3/5 = 60 percent of the variance. Projections on it: -1.414, -1.414, 0, 0, 2.828. PCA is also computed through the SVD of the centered data matrix. Use it for visualization, compression and decorrelation, always fitting on training data only.\n\n## Chapter 8. Neural Networks\n\n### 8.1 The neuron and the multilayer perceptron\nA neuron computes a = φ(w·x + b). Stacking layers gives h = φ(W₁x + b₁), ŷ = W₂h + b₂. Without a non-linearity φ the stack collapses to one linear map. Common activations: sigmoid, tanh, **ReLU** max(0, z) (the default, cheap and non-saturating for z > 0), leaky ReLU, GELU. A network with one hidden layer can approximate any continuous function on a compact set (universal approximation), but depth makes this efficient.\n\n**Forward pass example (ReLU).** W₁ = [[1, 2], [-1, 1]], b₁ = (0, 1), W₂ = (2, -1), b₂ = 0.5. For x = (1, 2): pre-activations (5, 2), hidden (5, 2), output 2×5 - 2 + 0.5 = 8.5. For x = (1, -2): pre-activations (-3, -2), both ReLUs output 0, so the output is 0.5 and the gradient to W₁ is zero: this is a **dead ReLU**.\n\n### 8.2 Backpropagation\nBackpropagation is the chain rule applied efficiently on the computation graph. For each layer, compute the local gradient and multiply by the gradient arriving from above. Steps: forward pass storing activations; compute the loss; backward pass computing ∂L/∂(each parameter).\n\n**Worked example.** Network 2-2-1 with sigmoid hidden and output units, squared loss L = ½(o - t)². W₁ = [[0.5, -0.4], [0.3, 0.8]], b₁ = (0.1, -0.2), W₂ = (0.7, -0.6), b₂ = 0.05, input x = (1, 0), target t = 1.\n- Forward: hidden pre-activations (0.6, 0.1) give h = (0.6457, 0.5250); output pre-activation 0.7(0.6457) - 0.6(0.5250) + 0.05 = 0.187 gives o = 0.5466; L = 0.1028.\n- Output delta: δ_o = (o - t) o (1 - o) = (-0.4534)(0.2478) = -0.1124.\n- ∂L/∂W₂ = δ_o h = (-0.0725, -0.0590); ∂L/∂b₂ = -0.1124.\n- Hidden deltas: δ_h = δ_o W₂ ⊙ h(1 - h) = (-0.0180, 0.0168).\n- ∂L/∂W₁ = δ_h xᵀ = [[-0.0180, 0], [0.0168, 0]] (the second column is zero because x₂ = 0); ∂L/∂b₁ = (-0.0180, 0.0168).\nThese values match PyTorch autograd to four decimals. Always **gradient check** a hand-written backward pass against finite differences.\n\n**Vanishing and exploding gradients.** Products of many small derivatives (sigmoid derivative is at most 0.25) shrink the gradient exponentially with depth. Remedies: ReLU-family activations, careful initialization (Xavier for tanh, He for ReLU), normalization layers, residual connections.\n\n### 8.3 Optimizers\n- **SGD with momentum**: v ← βv + g, w ← w - ηv. On f = w² from w = 1 with η = 0.1, β = 0.9: v = 2, 3.4, 3.98 and w = 0.8, 0.46, 0.062. Momentum accelerates along consistent directions.\n- **Adam**: keeps running estimates m (mean of gradients) and v (mean of squared gradients), with bias correction, and steps by η m̂/(√v̂ + ε). On f = w² from w = 1 with η = 0.1: w = 0.900, 0.800, 0.702. The first Adam step is essentially η times the sign of the gradient. Typical defaults: β₁ = 0.9, β₂ = 0.999, ε = 1e-8, η = 1e-3.\n- Use a **learning-rate schedule** (step decay, cosine, warmup) and **gradient clipping** for recurrent and transformer models.\n\n### 8.4 Regularization for networks\n**Weight decay** (L2), **dropout** (randomly zero units with probability p during training and rescale), **early stopping** (stop when validation loss rises), **data augmentation**, and **batch normalization**. Batch norm normalizes each feature over the mini-batch: for activations 2, 4, 6, 8, the mean is 5 and the variance 5, so the normalized values are -1.342, -0.447, 0.447, 1.342, followed by a learned scale γ and shift β. It stabilizes and speeds training. At test time it uses running averages.\n\n### 8.5 Convolutional neural networks\nA convolution slides a small kernel over the input, sharing weights and exploiting locality, so the parameter count is far smaller than a fully connected layer. Output size = ⌊(n + 2p - k)/s⌋ + 1 for input n, kernel k, padding p, stride s. A 32 input with k = 5, p = 0, s = 1 gives 28; a 28 input with k = 3, p = 1, s = 2 gives 14. Parameters of a conv layer: k × k × C_in × C_out + C_out; a 3×3 convolution from 3 to 16 channels has 448. In the classic LeNet layout, conv 1→6 with 5×5 has 156 parameters, conv 6→16 has 2,416, and a dense 400→120 layer has 48,120. A fully connected 784-128-10 network has 101,770 parameters.\n\n**Example.** Image [[1,2,0],[0,1,3],[2,1,0]] with kernel [[1,0],[0,-1]] (no padding, stride 1, cross-correlation as deep-learning libraries do): the output is [[0, -1], [-1, 1]]. Max pooling 2×2 on [[1,3,2,0],[4,2,1,1],[0,1,5,2],[2,3,1,4]] gives [[4, 2], [3, 5]]. Pooling reduces resolution and gives some translation invariance.\n\n### 8.6 Sequences and attention\nRecurrent networks (RNN, LSTM, GRU) carry a hidden state through time but train slowly and forget long-range context. **Attention** lets each position look at all others: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k) V. The **transformer** stacks attention and feed-forward blocks with residual connections and layer normalization; it replaced recurrence in language and is now used for vision and audio. Large language models are transformers trained to predict the next token, then fine-tuned with human feedback.\n\n## Chapter 9. Practical Training and Debugging\n\n1. Start tiny: overfit a batch of 10 examples. If you cannot, there is a bug.\n2. Check the loss at initialization: for K-way softmax it should be about ln K (ln 10 = 2.30).\n3. Plot training and validation loss. Both high: underfit (bigger model, train longer). Training low, validation high: overfit (regularize, more data).\n4. Change one thing at a time; fix random seeds; log everything.\n5. Class imbalance: reweight the loss, resample, or choose the metric and threshold carefully.\n6. Search hyperparameters on a log scale; random search beats grid search.\n\n## Chapter 10. Deployment and MLOps\n\n- **Serving**: batch prediction versus online APIs (REST/gRPC); watch latency and throughput.\n- **Model compression**: quantization (float32 → int8 gives 4× smaller), pruning, distillation; export to ONNX, TensorFlow Lite or TorchScript for edge devices.\n- **Monitoring**: input data drift, concept drift, prediction distribution, accuracy once labels arrive. Retrain on a schedule or trigger.\n- **Reproducibility**: version code, data and models; keep a model card documenting data, intended use and limitations.\n- **Responsible AI**: audit for bias across groups (compare error rates by subgroup), protect privacy, explain predictions where decisions affect people, and keep a human in the loop for high-stakes use.\n\n## Chapter 11. Common Mistakes Checklist\n- Fitting the scaler or PCA on all the data before splitting (leakage).\n- Tuning on the test set; reporting the best of many runs.\n- Using accuracy on imbalanced data.\n- Forgetting to standardize features for k-NN, SVM, ridge and gradient descent.\n- Learning rate too high (loss goes to NaN) or no shuffling of training data.\n- Comparing models without a baseline or without variance across seeds.\n- Mixing up cross-correlation and convolution, or forgetting that PyTorch loss functions expect logits, not probabilities.\n","plan":"# Machine Learning and AI: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 12 hours per week (3 reading or lectures, 5 to 7 coding and problems, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Machine learning is learned by building: implement each algorithm once from scratch in NumPy, then use the library version (scikit-learn, PyTorch) and check they agree.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Aurélien Géron, *Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras and TensorFlow* (practical first read).\n- Christopher Bishop, *Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning* (probabilistic view; free PDF from the author).\n- Hastie, Tibshirani and Friedman, *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (free PDF) and the lighter *An Introduction to Statistical Learning*.\n- Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, *Deep Learning* (free online); Zhang et al., *Dive into Deep Learning* (d2l.ai, runnable code).\n- Murphy, *Probabilistic Machine Learning: An Introduction* (free draft online).\n- Sutton and Barto, *Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction* (free online) for the RL chapters.\n\n## Free courses\n- Andrew Ng, *Machine Learning Specialization* (Coursera) and the Stanford CS229 notes.\n- Stanford CS231n (convolutional networks), CS224n (language), CS229 lecture videos.\n- fast.ai *Practical Deep Learning for Coders*; Andrej Karpathy's *Neural Networks: Zero to Hero* (build backprop and a transformer from scratch).\n- 3Blue1Brown's neural network series; StatQuest (Josh Starmer) for intuition about every classical method.\n- Kaggle Learn micro-courses and Kaggle competitions for practice on real datasets.\n\n## Tools\nPython 3, NumPy, pandas, Matplotlib, scikit-learn, PyTorch, Jupyter or Colab. Learn Git and a notebook-to-script workflow early.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Foundations and workflow.** Supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning; loss and risk; train/validation/test; cross-validation; data leakage. Lab: load a dataset with pandas, build a baseline, plot distributions.\n\n**Week 2: Linear regression.** Normal equations, gradient descent, learning rate, feature scaling. Lab: implement both solvers in NumPy; reproduce the notes' worked example (b = 1.5, w = 0.8).\n\n**Week 3: Bias, variance and regularization.** Polynomial features, ridge, lasso, cross-validated hyperparameters. Lab: reproduce the degree 1, 3, 9 experiment and plot validation curves.\n\n**Week 4: Logistic regression and metrics.** Sigmoid, cross-entropy, softmax, confusion matrix, precision, recall, ROC and PR curves, thresholds. Lab: train on an imbalanced dataset and pick a threshold by cost.\n\n**Week 5: k-NN, naive Bayes and decision trees.** Distance metrics, scaling, entropy and Gini, pruning. Lab: build a tree splitter from scratch and compare it with scikit-learn.\n\n**Week 6: SVMs, kernels and ensembles.** Margin, soft margin, RBF kernel; bagging, random forests, boosting. Lab: compare random forest, gradient boosting and a linear model on a tabular dataset.\n\n**Week 7: Midterm review and unsupervised learning.** k-means, choosing k, silhouette, hierarchical clustering, PCA via SVD. Lab: PCA on a face or digits dataset; cluster and visualize.\n\n**Week 8: Neural networks I.** Neurons, MLPs, activations, forward pass, universal approximation. Lab: forward pass by hand and in NumPy; check shapes.\n\n**Week 9: Neural networks II: backpropagation.** Chain rule on graphs, vanishing gradients, initialization. Lab: write backprop for a 2-layer net and verify with gradient checking and PyTorch autograd.\n\n**Week 10: Optimization and regularization for deep learning.** SGD, momentum, Adam, schedules, batch normalization, dropout, weight decay, early stopping. Lab: compare optimizers on one problem; plot loss curves.\n\n**Week 11: Convolutional networks.** Convolution arithmetic, pooling, ResNets, transfer learning, augmentation. Lab: train a small CNN on MNIST or CIFAR-10, then fine-tune a pretrained model.\n\n**Week 12: Sequences, attention and transformers.** RNNs, LSTMs, attention, transformer blocks, language models, embeddings. Lab: implement scaled dot-product attention; follow Karpathy's mini-GPT.\n\n**Week 13: Deployment, MLOps and responsible AI.** Quantization, pruning, ONNX/TFLite, monitoring and drift, fairness audits, model cards. Lab: export a model to ONNX, quantize it to int8, and measure size, latency and accuracy.\n\n**Week 14: Revision and project.** Past exam questions, end-to-end project: data, baseline, tuned model, error analysis, report. Present the result with its limitations.\n\n## Assessment pattern used in this course\nThree assignments (regression, classification and classical models, neural networks and deployment), a class test, and a three-hour final. Practise by redoing every worked example in the notes first on paper and then in code.\n\n## Study tips\n- Always start with the simplest baseline and a clean validation scheme before trying a complex model.\n- Plot everything: the data, the learning curves, the errors. Most bugs are visible in a plot.\n- Keep a lab notebook: hypotheses, settings, seeds, results.\n- Read one paper per week after Week 8 (ResNet, Adam, Batch Norm, Attention Is All You Need).\n- Beware of leakage and of tuning on the test set; both give results that vanish in deployment.\n","test":"# Machine Learning and AI: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed, no notes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Useful values: σ(0) = 0.5, σ(0.625) = 0.6514, ln 2 = 0.693, log₂ values as needed.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Supervised learning requires\nA) only unlabeled data  B) labeled examples (inputs with targets)  C) a reward signal  D) no data\n\n**2.** A model with very low training error but high test error is\nA) underfitting  B) well calibrated  C) overfitting  D) unbiased\n\n**3.** The test set should be used\nA) repeatedly to tune hyperparameters  B) once, to report final performance  C) for training  D) to initialize weights\n\n**4.** The normal equations for least squares are\nA) XᵀX w = Xᵀy  B) X w = y always  C) w = Xᵀy  D) XXᵀ = I\n\n**5.** If the gradient descent learning rate is far too large, the loss typically\nA) reaches zero at once  B) decreases smoothly  C) stays constant  D) oscillates or diverges\n\n**6.** The ridge (L2) penalty is proportional to\nA) the sum of the absolute weights  B) the sum of the squared weights  C) the number of nonzero weights  D) the largest weight\n\n**7.** The lasso (L1) penalty tends to produce\nA) larger weights  B) identical weights  C) sparse weights, some exactly zero  D) negative weights only\n\n**8.** The sigmoid function σ(0) equals\nA) 0  B) 0.5  C) 1  D) 0.25\n\n**9.** The loss normally used to train logistic regression is\nA) binary cross-entropy (log loss)  B) hinge loss only  C) the number of clusters  D) mean absolute percentage error\n\n**10.** Precision is\nA) TP/(TP + FN)  B) TN/(TN + FP)  C) TP/(TP + FP)  D) (TP + TN)/N\n\n**11.** Recall (sensitivity) is\nA) TP/(TP + FN)  B) TP/(TP + FP)  C) FP/(FP + TN)  D) 1 - accuracy\n\n**12.** For a highly imbalanced classification task, a more informative summary than accuracy is\nA) the number of features  B) the training time  C) the learning rate  D) precision, recall or the F1 score\n\n**13.** k-nearest neighbours is sensitive to the scale of features, so you should\nA) never normalize  B) standardize the features  C) use only one feature  D) use k = N\n\n**14.** The entropy of a node with a 50/50 class split is\nA) 0 bits  B) 0.5 bits  C) 1 bit  D) 2 bits\n\n**15.** Bagging mainly reduces\nA) variance  B) bias only  C) the number of training examples  D) the label noise\n\n**16.** The k-means algorithm minimizes\nA) the number of clusters  B) the within-cluster sum of squared distances  C) the between-cluster distance  D) the cross-entropy\n\n**17.** The first principal component is\nA) the feature with the largest mean  B) a random direction  C) the direction of smallest variance  D) the direction of maximum variance\n\n**18.** ReLU(-3) equals\nA) 0  B) -3  C) 3  D) 1\n\n**19.** The outputs of a softmax layer\nA) can be negative  B) are always integers  C) are positive and sum to 1  D) sum to the number of classes\n\n**20.** Backpropagation computes gradients using\nA) random search  B) the chain rule on the computation graph  C) the median of the data  D) finite differences only\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Linear regression (10 marks)** Data (x, y): (1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 5). (a) Find the least-squares line ŷ = b + w x [4]. (b) Compute the residuals, SS_res and R² [4]. (c) Find the ridge slope with λ = 2 penalizing only the slope [2].\n\n**B2. Classification metrics (10 marks)** A classifier is tested on 200 cases and produces TP = 30, FP = 20, FN = 10, TN = 140. (a) Compute accuracy, precision, recall, specificity and F1 [6]. (b) After raising the decision threshold the counts become TP = 22, FP = 5, FN = 18, TN = 155. Compute the new precision, recall and accuracy, and describe the trade-off [4].\n\n**B3. Backpropagation (10 marks)** A single sigmoid neuron has input x = 2, weight w = 0.5, bias b = -1 and target t = 1, with loss L = ½(a - t)². (a) Compute z, the output a and the loss [3]. (b) Compute ∂L/∂w and ∂L/∂b [4]. (c) Apply one gradient step with η = 1 and compute the new output and loss [3].\n\n**B4. Convolution and model size (10 marks)** (a) An input of size 5×5 is convolved with a 3×3 kernel. Give the output size for (stride 1, padding 0), (stride 1, padding 1) and (stride 2, padding 0) [3]. (b) How many parameters has a 3×3 convolution layer from 16 to 32 channels with bias? A 5×5 layer from 3 to 8 channels? [3] (c) Explain two reasons why overfitting happens and give two remedies [4].\n\n## Answer key and solutions\n\n### Section A\n1. B 2. C 3. B 4. A 5. D 6. B 7. C 8. B 9. A 10. C 11. A 12. D 13. B 14. C 15. A 16. B 17. D 18. A 19. C 20. B\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1: x̄ = 2, ȳ = 11/3 = 3.667.\nStep 2: S_xy = (-1)(-1.667) + 0 + (1)(1.333) = 3; S_xx = 2.\nStep 3: Slope **w = 1.5**, intercept b = 3.667 - 3 = **0.667**.\n(b) Step 1: Fitted values 2.167, 3.667, 5.167; residuals **-0.167, 0.333, -0.167** (sum 0).\nStep 2: SS_res = 0.0278 + 0.1111 + 0.0278 = **0.1667**.\nStep 3: SS_tot = 2.778 + 0.111 + 1.778 = 4.667.\nStep 4: **R² = 1 - 0.1667/4.667 = 0.964**.\n(c) Slope = S_xy/(S_xx + λ) = 3/(2 + 2) = **0.75**, intercept = 3.667 - 0.75 × 2 = 2.167.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: Accuracy = 170/200 = **0.85**.\nStep 2: Precision = 30/50 = **0.60**.\nStep 3: Recall = 30/40 = **0.75**.\nStep 4: Specificity = 140/160 = **0.875**.\nStep 5: F1 = 2(0.6)(0.75)/1.35 = **0.667**.\n(b) Precision = 22/27 = **0.815**, recall = 22/40 = **0.55**, accuracy = 177/200 = **0.885**. Raising the threshold made the classifier more conservative: fewer false alarms (higher precision) but more missed positives (lower recall). The accuracy rose only because negatives dominate; the right threshold depends on the relative cost of false positives and false negatives.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) z = 0.5 × 2 - 1 = **0**; a = σ(0) = **0.5**; L = ½(0.5 - 1)² = **0.125**.\n(b) ∂L/∂z = (a - t) a (1 - a) = (-0.5)(0.25) = -0.125. ∂L/∂w = -0.125 × x = **-0.25**; ∂L/∂b = **-0.125**.\n(c) w = 0.5 + 0.25 = **0.75**, b = -1 + 0.125 = **-0.875**. New z = 1.5 - 0.875 = 0.625, a = σ(0.625) = **0.6514**, L = ½(0.3486)² = **0.0608**: the loss decreased.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Output = ⌊(5 + 2p - 3)/s⌋ + 1: stride 1, padding 0 → **3×3**; stride 1, padding 1 → **5×5** (\"same\" size); stride 2, padding 0 → **2×2**.\n(b) 3×3×16×32 + 32 = **4,640**; 5×5×3×8 + 8 = **608**.\n(c) Overfitting happens when the model is too flexible for the amount of data and when the data are noisy or unrepresentative, so the model memorizes noise. Remedies: more data or augmentation, regularization (weight decay, dropout), early stopping, a simpler model, ensembling, cross-validated hyperparameter choice.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Supervised learning requires","options":["only unlabeled data","labeled examples (inputs with targets)","a reward signal","no data"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A model with very low training error but high test error is","options":["underfitting","well calibrated","overfitting","unbiased"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The test set should be used","options":["repeatedly to tune hyperparameters","once, to report final performance","for training","to initialize weights"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The normal equations for least squares are","options":["XᵀX w = Xᵀy","X w = y always","w = Xᵀy","XXᵀ = I"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"If the gradient descent learning rate is far too large, the loss typically","options":["reaches zero at once","decreases smoothly","stays constant","oscillates or diverges"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The ridge (L2) penalty is proportional to","options":["the sum of the absolute weights","the sum of the squared weights","the number of nonzero weights","the largest weight"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The lasso (L1) penalty tends to produce","options":["larger weights","identical weights","sparse weights, some exactly zero","negative weights only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The sigmoid function σ(0) equals","options":["0","0.5","1","0.25"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The loss normally used to train logistic regression is","options":["binary cross-entropy (log loss)","hinge loss only","the number of clusters","mean absolute percentage error"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Precision is","options":["TP/(TP + FN)","TN/(TN + FP)","TP/(TP + FP)","(TP + TN)/N"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Recall (sensitivity) is","options":["TP/(TP + FN)","TP/(TP + FP)","FP/(FP + TN)","1 - accuracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"For a highly imbalanced classification task, a more informative summary than accuracy is","options":["the number of features","the training time","the learning rate","precision, recall or the F1 score"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"k-nearest neighbours is sensitive to the scale of features, so you should","options":["never normalize","standardize the features","use only one feature","use k = N"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The entropy of a node with a 50/50 class split is","options":["0 bits","0.5 bits","1 bit","2 bits"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Bagging mainly reduces","options":["variance","bias only","the number of training examples","the label noise"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The k-means algorithm minimizes","options":["the number of clusters","the within-cluster sum of squared distances","the between-cluster distance","the cross-entropy"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The first principal component is","options":["the feature with the largest mean","a random direction","the direction of smallest variance","the direction of maximum variance"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"ReLU(-3) equals","options":["0","-3","3","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The outputs of a softmax layer","options":["can be negative","are always integers","are positive and sum to 1","sum to the number of classes"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Backpropagation computes gradients using","options":["random search","the chain rule on the computation graph","the median of the data","finite differences only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Compared with a simple model, a very flexible model typically has","options":["higher bias and higher variance","lower bias and higher variance","higher bias and lower variance","lower bias and lower variance"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The main purpose of k-fold cross-validation is to","options":["increase the training set","remove outliers","estimate generalization performance and choose hyperparameters","speed up training"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which of these is an example of data leakage?","options":["fitting a scaler on the whole dataset before splitting","fitting the scaler on training data only","shuffling the training set","using a held-out test set"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Standardizing features helps gradient descent because it","options":["removes all noise","reduces the number of parameters","guarantees zero training error","makes the loss surface better conditioned so one learning rate suits all directions"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Compared with ridge, the lasso penalty","options":["can set weights exactly to zero","never shrinks weights","is always faster to compute","cannot be used with regression"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For softmax with cross-entropy, the gradient of the loss with respect to the logits is","options":["y - z","p²","p - y (probabilities minus the one-hot label)","log p"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An ROC AUC of 0.5 means the classifier","options":["is perfect","ranks no better than chance","is always wrong","has 50 percent precision"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Gini impurity of a pure node is","options":["0","0.5","1","undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A random forest decorrelates its trees by","options":["pruning all trees","using one deep tree","training on test data","bootstrap sampling and random feature subsets at each split"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Gradient boosting builds trees","options":["independently and averages them","at random","sequentially, each fitting the errors (negative gradient) of the ensemble so far","only on the test set"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"k-means++ initialization is intended to","options":["choose k automatically","spread out the initial centroids to avoid poor local optima","guarantee the global optimum","remove outliers"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"When using PCA before a classifier, the PCA should be fitted on","options":["the training data only","the test data","the whole dataset","the labels"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A \"dead\" ReLU unit is one that","options":["has weights equal to one","has a large positive output","is used in the output layer","outputs zero for essentially all inputs, so it receives no gradient"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"He initialization is designed for","options":["sigmoid units","linear regression","ReLU units","k-means"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Stacking many sigmoid layers causes vanishing gradients mainly because","options":["the weights are too large","the sigmoid derivative is at most 0.25, so products shrink exponentially","there is no bias","the loss is not differentiable"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Adam optimizer combines","options":["momentum-like first moments with per-parameter scaling by second moments","random restarts with pruning","bagging with boosting","PCA with SGD"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Batch normalization normalizes each feature","options":["over the whole training set once","across the layers","over the labels","over the current mini-batch, followed by a learned scale and shift"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"At test time, dropout","options":["randomly zeroes units again","doubles the weights","uses all units (with the training-time scaling convention already accounted for)","is replaced by L1"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Scaled dot-product attention computes","options":["argmax(QKᵀ) V","softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k) V","QKᵀ + V","ReLU(QV)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"When the relationship P(y | x) changes over time (for example fraud tactics evolve) this is called","options":["concept drift","covariate invariance","overfitting","regularization"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
